meta {
  name: Get token
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{custodianUrl}}/token
  body: json
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "scope": "{{scope}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.body && res.body.access_token) {
    bru.setEnvVar("accessToken", res.body.access_token);
  }
}

docs {
  The full L2 flow in one call: the key custodian signs the private_key_jwt
  assertion AND runs the client_credentials exchange at the auth server, then
  returns the token response. The access token is saved to `accessToken` and
  carries this party's SMART scopes and organization_reference claim.

  Body fields are optional: `scope` ({{scope}}) requests specific SMART scopes —
  omit it to fall back to the custodian's DEFAULT_SCOPE. You can also pass RFC
  9396 `authorization_details`, e.g.
  [{"type": "http://fhir.ch/ig/ch-umzh-connect/context", "identifier": "ServiceRequest/<id>"}].
  The client is always the custodian's CLIENT_ID; any other field is a 400.
  The AS still decides what it grants.
}
